<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">What&#8217;s new</h1><p class="ref-lede">What the Institute has added and changed, newest first, with the smaller changes of each week in a line, and how many pages appeared in the last 30 days. Follow it with the <a href="/whats-new.xml">feed</a>.</p></header>
<section class="wn-notes" aria-labelledby="wn-notes-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="wn-notes-t">Release notes</h2><ol class="wn-list"><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-16">16 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">The best-known entries, told in more detail</h3><p class="wn-s">More than 12,500 of the encyclopedia’s best-known entries (countries, cities, peoples and the people readers look for most) now go beyond their first paragraph: each has two or three further sections on its setting, its history and what remains, written from its source article and checked against it. This is the start of a longer effort to tell every entry in full.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/">The encyclopedia</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-16">16 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Past games: every earlier day of the daily games</h3><p class="wn-s">A new page lists every day of the eight daily games since each began, as a calendar. Choose a game and a day to play that day’s puzzle; the days already played show their result. The home page and the profile pages were also straightened: edges, cards and boxes of one row now line up.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/past-games">Past games</a> <a href="/games">History games</a></p></li><li class="wn-note wn-small"><p class="wn-d">Week of <time datetime="2026-11-16">16 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Smaller changes</h3><p class="wn-s">Corrections and bug fixes.</p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-15">15 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Sources on nearly four thousand entries</h3><p class="wn-s">3,933 encyclopedia entries now end with a Sources section naming the scholarly works their Wikipedia article lists or cites, 9,518 works in all; a further 4,400 name the article they are written from. Only works about the subject itself, from scholarly publishers, are listed.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/method">How the Institute works</a> <a href="/corrections">Corrections</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-15">15 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Every great speech now has its words</h3><p class="wn-s">All 115 speeches now carry a text. Churchill's We Shall Fight on the Beaches, Mandela's statement from the dock, Jinnah's address to Pakistan's Constituent Assembly, Lloyd George at Limehouse, Stanton's Solitude of Self and Charles I on the scaffold are printed complete; the others have the passages that free sources give, each naming where its words come from.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/speeches/">Great speeches</a> <a href="/speeches/churchill-we-shall-fight">We Shall Fight on the Beaches</a> <a href="/speeches/mandela-statement-from-the-dock">Mandela's statement from the dock</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-14">14 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Wars and their battles, and the words of five founders</h3><p class="wn-s">A new page lists every war in the encyclopedia by region, oldest first, with its battles, sieges and treaties. The quotations page counts its speakers again and now holds sayings of Muhammad, verses of the Buddha, and passages of Laozi, Moses and Guru Nanak, each from a named edition. Entries, profiles and state pages ask whether the page was useful.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/wars">Wars and their battles</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-14">14 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">How the Institute works, and a public list of corrections</h3><p class="wn-s">A new page sets out where each kind of page comes from, how drafts are written and checked against their sources, and the rules the texts follow. A second lists every correction of fact made so far, 774 of them, with what was wrong and what replaced it.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/method">How the Institute works</a> <a href="/corrections">Corrections</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Twenty more great lives, and pictures in the first twenty</h3><p class="wn-s">Twenty more lives told in full: Hammurabi, Ramesses II, Aristotle, Hannibal, Qin Shi Huang, Charlemagne, Harun al-Rashid, Rumi, Kublai Khan, Pachacuti, Joan of Arc, Martin Luther, Suleiman the Magnificent, Tokugawa Ieyasu, Isaac Newton, Catherine the Great, George Washington, Toussaint Louverture, Liliʻuokalani and Haile Selassie. The sections of all forty lives now carry pictures.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=life">Great lives</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Great lives: twenty people told in full</h3><p class="wn-s">A new kind of profile: twenty lives from Cyrus the Great, Confucius and Ashoka to Elizabeth I, Simón Bolívar and Nelson Mandela, each with its early life, rise, deeds, opponents and critics, last years and legacy, key events and references.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=life">Great lives</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Rulers on the profiles, and who was born near any place</h3><p class="wn-s">The profiles of 174 states and empires now list their rulers and leaders among the people of the encyclopedia, each a person whose own entry names them as that state’s ruler. On the atlas, “What was here?” also lists the people born within 250 km of the place you choose.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/atlas">The atlas</a> <a href="/profiles">Profiles</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Born Where? A new game on the globe</h3><p class="wn-s">A person of history is named: turn the globe and drop your pin where they were born. Five people from five parts of the world, with the birthplace and the distance shown after each guess.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/games#hunt-born">Born Where?</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">In their words: quotations on the pages of those who spoke them</h3><p class="wn-s">The pages of more than five hundred people now carry their own words: some two thousand quotations, each taken from a named public-domain edition, with its work, translator and source. A new page lists the speakers by age.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Today at the Institute, and a bar for phones</h3><p class="wn-s">One page for the day: what happened on this day, who was born, the song of the day, the daily quiz and the eight daily games with your results, and history in the news. On phones a bar at the foot of the screen keeps Home, Search, Today, Play and the menu under your thumb.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/today">Today at the Institute</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Easier to find your way</h3><p class="wn-s">Long pages now carry a row of their sections under the header, so any part is one tap away. The profiles, the songs, the Kids themes and the longest country timelines open on a first screenful with “Show all”. On phones an entry begins with its text, not its picture. The home page has four ways in under the search, and one page, Explore Everything, shows every section with a box to find one.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/research">Explore Everything</a> <a href="/profiles">Profiles</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-11">11 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Where people were born, and four new open data tables</h3><p class="wn-s">The pages of some 7,500 people now say where they were born and died, with a dot on the map, and each country history lists the best-known people born there; the places are Wikidata’s. Open Data gains four tables to download: the people, the songs, the inventions, and the encyclopedia’s entries matched to Wikipedia and Wikidata.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/dataset">Open Data</a> <a href="/people/">People in History</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-11">11 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">History Now: the past in this week’s news</h3><p class="wn-s">A new page and a card on the home page: history and archaeology headlines from six publications, each beside the Institute’s page on its subject; the round anniversaries of the next thirty days; and what is new here. The headlines are refreshed twice a day and are not shown in Kids mode.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/now">History Now</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-11">11 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Finish the Line: a new daily game</h3><p class="wn-s">A few lines of a song from history, and four lines to choose from: which one comes next? Five songs a day, the same for everyone, from carols and anthems to hymns and sea shanties; songs in other languages have their English under every line.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/games#line">Finish the Line</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-11">11 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Quote Cipher: a new daily game</h3><p class="wn-s">A quotation from history with every letter swapped for another: work out the words square by square, then learn who said them and where. One cipher a day, the same for everyone, with a letter to reveal when you are stuck; Kids mode starts with three letters given.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/games#cipher">Quote Cipher</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-11">11 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">A front page for the games</h3><p class="wn-s">History Games now opens on a card for each of the fourteen games, with today’s daily games first and your result on each once you have played. New things are marked New in the menus, your birthday in history has its own place in the Play menu, and My Passport has two new stamps.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/games">History Games</a> <a href="/passport">My Passport</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-11">11 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Longer or Shorter? A new game</h3><p class="wn-s">Two states or empires of the past, each with its emblem: which lasted longer? The gaps narrow as your streak grows, from centuries to a couple of decades, and you have three lives. It has its own tab among the History Games.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/games#first-lasted">Longer or Shorter?</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-11">11 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">An Advent calendar of history, and your birthday in history</h3><p class="wn-s">Twenty-four doors for the days of December: behind each a festival of the dark season, a carol or something that happened on that day, opening one a day from 1 December. And on Today in History you can now choose your birthday, or any day, to see what happened on it, who was born and died on it, and share its poster.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/advent">Advent calendar</a> <a href="/today-in-history/">Today in History</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-12">12 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Midwinter and Christmas Day: two essays, three paths and a winter theme for children</h3><p class="wn-s">Two new essays in the Research Series: Midwinter, on how the world has marked the darkest days, from Saturnalia, Yule and Koliada to Dongzhi, Yalda Night and Inti Raymi; and Christmas Day in History, on the crownings, crossings and truces of 25 December, from Charlemagne in 800 to the Christmas truce of 1914. Three new reading paths follow midwinter festivals, the story of Christmas and winter at war. The Kids Corner has a Winter Festivals theme with its own Word Hunt.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a> <a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a> <a href="/paths/">Reading paths</a> <a href="/kids">Kids Corner</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-11">11 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">The months in history, midwinter festivals and thirteen carols</h3><p class="wn-s">Every month now has a page of its own: its best-known events, the people born and died in it, its festivals and its songs. Forty-nine new encyclopedia entries tell of the festivals of the dark season, from Saturnalia, Yule, Dongzhi and Yalda Night to Christmas, Hogmanay and the Chinese New Year, and of winter’s events, among them the Christmas truce of 1914, Washington’s crossing of the Delaware and the frost fairs on the Thames. Thirteen more carols join Songs in History, eleven with their words, from Personent hodie and Tu scendi dalle stelle, each with a new English translation, to Good King Wenceslas and The Twelve Days of Christmas.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/today-in-history/december">December in history</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> <a href="/songs/">Songs in History</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-10">10 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">New looks, and a winter one</h3><p class="wn-s">The Institute can now be read in more than night and parchment: Winter (midnight blue and frost, with a little snow you can switch off), Illuminated (lapis, vellum and the red ink of a manuscript), Marble and Bronze, and High contrast, each with a light and a dark side. Choose one under the sun or moon button at the top of any page. Left on Seasonal, the site puts on the winter look by itself from 1 December to 6 January.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/">Home</a></p></li><li class="wn-note wn-small"><p class="wn-d">Week of <time datetime="2026-11-09">9 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Smaller changes</h3><p class="wn-s">Content additions and improvements across the site; corrections and bug fixes.</p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-08">8 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Ruling houses, and where things were</h3><p class="wn-s">New: Ruling Houses and Dynasties, with each house’s members drawn on one scale of years (the Rurikids, the Habsburgs, the Mongol Borjigin, the Solomonic dynasty and many more). Places, battles and states in the encyclopedia now show where they lie, with a link to what was there on the atlas.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/dynasties">Ruling houses</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-08">8 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Support the Institute, and your privacy</h3><p class="wn-s">The Institute stays free, independent and without ads: readers who wish can now support it on Ko-fi. New pages say how the site uses data (Analytics only with your consent, never in Kids mode) and how its texts, data and maps may be reused.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/support">Support the Institute</a> <a href="/privacy">Privacy</a> <a href="/terms">Terms and licences</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-08">8 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Every entry, across the Institute</h3><p class="wn-s">Encyclopedia entries now quote what the rest of the Institute says about them: up to six sentences from the profiles, essays, social ladders, speeches, songs, day pages and state histories that name them, each linking its page.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulpian">Ulpian</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/">Encyclopedia</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-08">8 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Stories of faiths and movements for Kids mode</h3><p class="wn-s">In Kids mode, 61 more profiles open with a short picture story that can be read aloud: the 30 religions, from Buddhism and the faith of ancient Egypt to Sikhism and Yoruba religion, and 31 movements, from abolitionism and the Luddites to Solidarity and the Velvet Revolution, every sentence drawn from the profile itself.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism</a> <a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism</a> <a href="/kids">Kids Corner</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-08">8 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Test yourself on paper</h3><p class="wn-s">The questions at the foot of the profiles, the century pages and the reading paths now print as a worksheet: lettered answers, a line for the name, and the answer key with the reasons on a page of its own, for a quick check in class.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/empire/roman-empire#test-yourself">Roman Empire</a> <a href="/teachers/">Teachers' Corner</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-08">8 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Read further on Wikipedia</h3><p class="wn-s">Some 27,000 encyclopedia entries now link the Wikipedia article on the same subject, and its Wikidata item, each match checked by the picture, the years or the text rather than the name alone; entries dated to a day link that day in Today in History; and 25 entries lost a picture taken from a namesake, so the village of Kanye no longer shows Kanye West, nor the Mongol prince Duden the German dictionary.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/kanye">Kanye</a> <a href="/encyclopedia/">Encyclopedia</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-06">6 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Great works</h3><p class="wn-s">A new kind of profile: twenty great works of writing and building, from the Epic of Gilgamesh, the Code of Hammurabi and the Great Pyramid of Giza to the Divine Comedy, the Popol Vuh, Angkor Wat, the churches of Lalibela, Machu Picchu and the Taj Mahal, each with how it was made, received, survived and remembered.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=work">Great works</a> <a href="/work/parthenon">The Parthenon</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-06">6 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Picture stories for Kids</h3><p class="wn-s">In Kids mode, every one of the 65 culture profiles now opens with a short picture story that the page can read aloud, from the Natufians and the Indus cities to the Inuit, the Māori and the San today; the Kids Corner lists them all.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/kids#stories">Stories of the peoples</a> <a href="/culture/vikings">The story of the Vikings</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-06">6 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">People by region</h3><p class="wn-s">Nine new pages of People in History, one for each region of the world, with the best known first and then century by century; and 1,252 people placed in their right region, checked against where they were born, died and worked.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/people/europe">People of Europe</a> <a href="/people/americas">People of the Americas</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-06">6 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Lyrics from Genius</h3><p class="wn-s">Ninety-one songs whose words the Institute does not print, most of them still in copyright, now offer their lyrics from Genius: a button under The words opens Genius's own embed, shown under its licence. Nothing loads from Genius until you press it.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/songs/wind-of-change">Wind of Change</a> <a href="/songs/">Songs in History</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-06">6 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Easier to read, easier to use</h3><p class="wn-s">An accessibility pass to the WCAG 2.2 AA standard: faint grey and gold labels are now drawn at full strength, links inside running text are underlined as well as coloured, maps that hold links read as groups of links to screen readers, small picture-credit links are easier to tap, and wide tables in the essays can be scrolled from the keyboard.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/about">About the Institute</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-06">6 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">New daily game: Six Degrees</h3><p class="wn-s">A new daily puzzle in History Games: get from one encyclopedia entry to another, from Cleopatra to the printing press, by following the gold links in each entry's description. Every click counts, and the shortest route is shown at the end. It joins the week's results in My Week of Games.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/games#six">Six Degrees</a> <a href="/weekly">My Week of Games</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-05">5 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Four new essays, eight Kids themes, 208 game pictures</h3><p class="wn-s">Four new essays in the Research Series read the Institute's own collections side by side: Faiths on the Move (how thirty religions spread, met and ended), Who Invented It First? (what 291 inventions show about firsts and credit), From Ruler to Enslaved (fifty social ladders compared) and The Written Word (how thirty-two languages were written, carried and revived). The Kids Corner and Word Hunt have eight new themes, from the Mongols and the Silk Road to the Renaissance and the Industrial Revolution; the picture games have 208 new pictures, and the Picture Puzzle a new set, Great Buildings.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/research-series">Research Series</a> <a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a> <a href="/kids">Kids Corner</a> <a href="/games">History Games</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-05">5 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Social ladders: fifty societies</h3><p class="wn-s">Ten more social ladders, fifty in all: Ming China, Kievan Rus' and the bondsmen of the Russkaya Pravda, the Khmer Empire at Angkor, Majapahit, the Delhi Sultanate under the Tughluqs, Aksum under Ezana, the Zulu Kingdom, the Haudenosaunee Confederacy, the Dutch Republic in its Golden Age and British India around 1900. Every rank has its picture, its estimated share and the way in and out of it.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/societies/">Social Ladders</a> <a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">The Haudenosaunee</a> <a href="/societies/british-raj">British India</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-05">5 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Ten more social ladders</h3><p class="wn-s">Ten more societies set out rank by rank, from ruler to enslaved: Qing China and Heian Japan, Ayutthaya under the sakdina system, Safavid Iran, Ptolemaic Egypt, Gaul before Caesar, the Asante Empire and the Kingdom of Dahomey, the Republic of Venice and the Soviet Union under Stalin. Each has its estimated shares, how people rose and fell, the place of women, a picture for every rank, and a chance to be born into it.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/societies/">Social Ladders</a> <a href="/societies/qing-china">Qing China</a> <a href="/societies/soviet-union">The Soviet Union</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Songs in History: 67 more</h3><p class="wn-s">Sixty-seven more songs, 406 in all: the Song of Songs, Psalm 104, the Hallel and the Song of Hannah, the Hymn to the Nile, the Tevaram, the Charyapada and the Bhaja Govindam, the anthems of Algeria, Tunisia, Morocco, Thailand, Uganda and Gujarat, Sari Gelin and Ya Mustafa, Sweet Mother and Meadowlands, the Queen's Prayer and Tinsagu nu Hana, spirituals, sea shanties and carols, and favourites from Funiculì, Funiculà to Take Me Out to the Ball Game. Each has its story, a performance, and, where they are free to print, its words.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/songs/">Songs in History</a> <a href="/songs/song-of-songs">Song of Songs</a> <a href="/songs/sari-gelin">Sari Gelin</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">38 more Songs in History</h3><p class="wn-s">339 songs now: the Ballad of Mulan, the Epic of Sundiata, the Song of Deborah and the Enūma Eliš; Byzantine hymns of Kassia and the Akathist; Ma'oz Tzur, Victimae paschali laudes, In dulci jubilo, Gaudete and Es ist ein Ros entsprungen; the anthems of Brazil, Norway, Sweden, Finland, Romania, the Czech lands, Wales, Portugal and Odisha; the songs of the Ghost Dance, Joshua Fit the Battle of Jericho, Saint Louis Blues and Solidarity Forever; Molly Malone, Drunken Sailor, Loch Lomond, The Three Ravens, Men of Harlech and Malbrough. 31 with their words in the original, many with an English translation, and a performance for 37.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/songs/">Songs in History</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Today's Number, a dice for surprises, and a play button</h3><p class="wn-s">The home page now shows a number a day from the data, with the page it comes from, and the Song of the Day plays where it stands. A dice in the header leaps to a random page of history. Reading paths print as a booklet, year pages now start at four things to read (162 more years), and the menu names the States Register and the Power Curves.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/">Home</a> <a href="/years/">Year by year</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">73 more inventions and discoveries</h3><p class="wn-s">The inventions tree now holds 291, most of them in the lanes that were thin: the sewing needle of Denisova Cave, farming at Kuk Swamp, wine, cheese and coffee, fired brick, Egyptian blue and lacquerware, the shadoof, the water clock, the sundial, the screw and the lathe, Zhang Heng's seismoscope, the wheelbarrow, the kite and the junk, the pencil, the barometer and the thermometer, canning, the stethoscope and blood transfusion, Morse code, Braille and the phonograph, the electric motor, alternating current, hydroelectricity, radar, the personal computer and the lithium-ion battery, each with its own encyclopedia entry.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/inventions">Inventions tree</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">35 more Great Speeches</h3><p class="wn-s">115 speeches now, from King Wu of Zhou at Muye to Lyndon B. Johnson's &quot;We shall overcome&quot;: Alcibiades for the Sicilian expedition, Cato on the Oppian law, Caesar on the Catilinarians, al-Hajjaj at Kufa, Richard II at Smithfield, Powhatan to John Smith, Galileo's abjuration, Logan's Lament, Burke for conciliation with America and against Warren Hastings, Robert Emmet from the dock, Chief Seattle, the House Divided, John Brown to the court, Itō Hirobumi's rising sun, Tagore on nationalism, Pearse at O'Donovan Rossa's grave, the Four Freedoms and the Marshall Plan. 31 with their full text, and new entries for 14 of their speakers.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/speeches/">Great speeches</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">557 more events in Today in History</h3><p class="wn-s">Every day of the year now has more to read: 3,679 events in all, among them the Battle of Badr, the Council of Nicaea, the Mongols at the walls of Kaifeng, the Coonan Cross Oath, the fall of Ayutthaya, Yi Sun-sin at Myeongnyang, the Treaty of Karlowitz, Dandara of Palmares and the Stockholm Bloodbath. Each is dated by the text of its own source.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/today-in-history/">Today in History</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">24 new reading paths</h3><p class="wn-s">Sixty-two paths now: new routes through the ideologies and forms of government, abolition and votes for women, living peoples and the cultures before the state, the ancient gods and the faiths of Persia, the ancient Near East, Mesoamerica and the Andes, West, East and Southern Africa, the Mongol world, Japan, Korea and Southeast Asia, the Ottomans and Russia, the nations of the nineteenth century and the inventions that changed the world.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/paths/">Reading paths</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Ideologies: a new kind of profile</h3><p class="wn-s">Twenty ideologies, each with its origins, core ideas, spread and variants, its record in power, the critiques made of it and its legacy: liberalism, conservatism, republicanism and nationalism; capitalism, mercantilism and imperialism; socialism, communism, anarchism, social democracy and Maoism; fascism; Legalism in ancient China; Kemalism, pan-Arabism, Hindutva, Islamism and Peronism; and feminism. Drawn from saved sources and checked line by line, with what was done in each one’s name stated plainly.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=ideology">Ideologies</a> <a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism</a> <a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism</a> <a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Twenty more movements</h3><p class="wn-s">Twenty movements, reforms and revolutions: the Counter-Reformation, Chartism, the Luddites and the Decembrists, Impressionism, Modernism and the Bauhaus, Solidarity, the Velvet Revolution and the environmental movement, the Taiping Rebellion, the May Fourth Movement, the Meiji Restoration and the Tanzimat, the Young Turks, Zionism and the Arab Spring, the Harlem Renaissance, Négritude and the Non-Aligned Movement.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=movement">Movements</a> <a href="/movement/chartism">Chartism</a> <a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> <a href="/movement/negritude">Négritude</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Twelve more religions and sixteen more languages</h3><p class="wn-s">Twelve religions with their spread on the faith maps: the religions of Mesopotamia, Canaan, Rome and the Celts, Slavic paganism and Mithraism, Tengrism, Bon and Chinese folk religion, and the Druze faith, Yazidism and Haitian Vodou. And sixteen languages: Pali, Tamil, Malay, Mongolian, Armenian, Georgian, Middle Persian, Ottoman Turkish, Old English, Gothic, Old Church Slavonic, Phoenician, Coptic, Ge'ez, Guarani and Hawaiian.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/faiths">Faith maps</a> <a href="/profiles?kind=religion">Religions</a> <a href="/profiles?kind=language">Languages</a> <a href="/religion/yazidism">Yazidism</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Twenty more cultures</h3><p class="wn-s">Profiles of twenty more peoples and cultures without a state of their own: living peoples (the San, Khoekhoe and Tuareg, the Inuit, Navajo, Lakota and Kalinago, and Aboriginal Australians) and the cultures known from archaeology, from the Clovis and Paracas cultures to La Tène, Hallstatt, Cucuteni–Trypillia, the Nuragic civilisation and the Iberians, and in Asia the Tocharians, Longshan, Hongshan, Erlitou and the Hoabinhian.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=culture">Cultures</a> <a href="/culture/san-people">San people</a> <a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians</a> <a href="/culture/nuragic-civilisation">Nuragic civilisation</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-03">3 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Eighty-one more state profiles</h3><p class="wn-s">Full profiles, with power curves, key facts, section pictures and gold links, for 81 more states of the register: from Ur, Lagash and the Old Kingdom of Egypt to the Qin, Sui and Northern Wei, the Huns, Hephthalites and Uyghurs, Phrygia, Colchis and Artaxiad Armenia, the Visigoths, Vandals and Great Moravia, the Aghlabids, Idrisids, Marinids and Zengids, Funan, Chenla, Champa, Medang and Malacca, Ife, Dagbon, Loango, Ifat and Alodia, Saint-Domingue, Colonial Brazil, the Republic of Texas and the Confederacy, and the modern republics from Hungary and Turkey to Rwanda, Singapore and East Timor.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles">Profiles</a> <a href="/empire/ur">Ur</a> <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon</a> <a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Two new essays: Before the State, and How Songs Travel</h3><p class="wn-s">Research Series 07 asks how peoples lived without kings, from the Natufians and the Jōmon to the five living cultures that have outlived the states around them; 08 follows the 300 songs of Songs in History from the oldest tablets to the anthems, the tunes that branched into other nations' songs, and what states and movements made of them. Also new in Songs in History: Chollima on the Wing, the song of North Korea's Chollima Movement, and an entry for the movement itself.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/essays/before-the-state">Before the State</a> <a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a> <a href="/research-series">Research Series</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Songs of states and causes, and a dozen new features</h3><p class="wn-s">Sixty-six songs of states, parties and causes join Songs in History, from Rule, Britannia! and the Soviet anthem to the songs of Mao's China, North Korea, fascist Italy and the Hong Kong protests, each told as history. New: compare any two profiles side by side; a timeline view of the profiles; Where Is This Song From?, a daily song game; Born on This Day on the home page; Next to read; search inside the songs' words; the encyclopedia filtered by era and by picture; levels in My Passport; an On This Day card for other websites; reading paths read aloud as a guided tour; this What's new page; worksheets on the cultures and the songs; and the Epic of Gilgamesh now in Sumerian.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/songs/">The songs</a> <a href="/compare">Compare two profiles</a> <a href="/games#songq">The song game</a> <a href="/teachers/">Worksheets</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Encyclopedia, offline reading, Meanwhile in the world</h3><p class="wn-s">229 songs and 16 forms of government have entries of their own; entries and songs show what else was happening in the world that year; the home page has a Song of the Day; pages already read open without a connection, and the site can be added to a phone's home screen.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/songs/">The songs</a> <a href="/governance">Forms of government</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Thirty new cultures</h3><p class="wn-s">The culture archive grows from 15 to 45, with more of Asia and South Asia: Mehrgarh, the Vedic civilisation, Sangam-age Tamilakam, Jōmon, Yayoi, Liangzhu, Sanxingdui, Dong Son, the Ainu and more, from the Natufians and the Yamnaya to the Taíno, the Thule people and the Māori.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=culture">Cultures</a></p></li><li class="wn-note wn-small"><p class="wn-d">Week of <time datetime="2026-11-02">2 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Smaller changes</h3><p class="wn-s">Content additions and improvements across the site; corrections and bug fixes.</p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-01">1 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Songs in History</h3><p class="wn-s">233 songs handed down through history, from a Sumerian hymn to songs of the Cold War: their story, their words in the original, romanized and in English where they are in the public domain, and a sung performance.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/songs/">Songs in History</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-10-31">31 October 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Forms of government</h3><p class="wn-s">Twenty forms of government, from absolute monarchy to the city-state and colonial rule, each with how it works, its history, its strengths and weaknesses; a hub that compares them and lets you design a state; and an essay, Who Should Rule?</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/governance">Forms of government</a> <a href="/essays/who-should-rule">The essay</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-10-31">31 October 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">The inventions tree</h3><p class="wn-s">218 inventions and discoveries in eleven lanes, from stone tools to the World Wide Web: what each built on, what it led to and who made it first.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/inventions">Inventions tree</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-10-30">30 October 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Social ladders</h3><p class="wn-s">How thirty societies ranked their people, ruler to enslaved, with how many stood on each rung; be born into one by chance, compare two, or put the ranks in order in a game.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/societies/">Social ladders</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-10-29">29 October 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Thirty cultures and movements, speeches and paths</h3><p class="wn-s">The first cultures and movements, 138 new encyclopedia entries, twenty more great speeches and ten more reading paths.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/speeches/">Great speeches</a> <a href="/paths/">Reading paths</a></p></li><li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-10-28">28 October 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Rise and Fall Race, Flashcards, Who Said It?</h3><p class="wn-s">Race empires through the centuries, learn with flashcards, guess who said a quotation, test yourself at the end of a profile, and see everything you have read on My timeline.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/race">Rise and Fall Race</a> <a href="/flashcards">Flashcards</a> <a href="/games">Games</a></p></li></ol></section>
